./LOGBOOK.md:15:- ROOT CAUSE: `neagent` previously received zero PlugInKit matches because both the development-signed host and embedded packet-tunnel provider omitted `com.apple.security.app-sandbox`. Adding App Sandbox to both exact targets made PlugInKit discover one provider at the expected embedded path after launch; Network Extension stayed exactly `packet-tunnel-provider`, with no App Groups or signed Keychain Sharing entitlement.
./LOGBOOK.md:36:- SCOPE EVIDENCE: Exactly four local provisioning profiles changed during the run and all four are the approved macOS development set; no unexpected, iOS, or distribution profile changed. The isolated harness declared no App Group, Keychain Sharing, iOS, distribution, or legacy-bundle mutation, and raw profiles/certificates were neither attached nor committed.
./LOGBOOK.md:43:- ACCESS ANOMALIES: Read-only Safari automation stalled and was terminated at exit 130 without page data. A named-profile `notarytool history` probe exited 1 because the default Keychain was locked; `TASK-260728-dveo1o` owns revalidation after unattended Keychain access is restored. No secret, credential, certificate private key, recovery code, or full device identifier was captured.
./LOGBOOK.md:48:- PRIVACY POLICY: Credential values and secret paths remain prohibited in repository, board resources, run logs, and shell history. Safe command-name-only history does not fail the ceremony; secret-bearing arguments, credential input, identifiers, account credentials, and key bytes still do.
./LOGBOOK.md:52:- EVIDENCE GAPS: Prompt-free `/usr/bin/codesign` probes demonstrate effective access but cannot prove the required Always Allow grants were performed in C1; an authenticated portal session does not prove two-factor completed during C1; two safe credential command-name lines in history mean literal per-command history suppression cannot be claimed. These facts cannot be repaired retrospectively by editing the outcome.
./LOGBOOK.md:57:- AUTHORITY: The owner confirmed an authenticated Apple Developer session for Relux Works, LLC and authority for revision `2026-07-28.r12`: exactly the four macOS App IDs, Network Extensions on all four, and one Mac Development profile per App ID with this Apple-silicon Mac registered. No App Group record/capability, Keychain access-group portal mutation, iOS App ID, distribution profile, or `works.relux.proxy` change is authorized.
./LOGBOOK.md:59:- PRIVACY: Targeted post-ceremony scans found zero secret/key-path/key-ID/issuer-ID/account-credential candidate files in the Git-scoped repository, board resources, run logs, and shell history, and zero key files in repository/board scope. History nevertheless contains one safe Sparkle generation command-name line and one safe Keychain-unlock command-name line, with no password flags; full history suppression therefore cannot be claimed.
./LOGBOOK.md:60:- ANOMALY: The older `TASK-260728-3a2dnr_ceremony-c1.md` planning artifact predates matrix rule G4/K2 and still describes two macOS IDs plus App Group/Keychain grants. It is historical and must not drive execution; the live task contract and accepted r12 matrix are authoritative.
./LOGBOOK.md:63:- CONFORMANCE: A live OpenSSH fixture now authenticates an in-memory Ed25519 credential through the adapter's async `SSHPublicKeyCredential.sign` callback, records an actual opaque-signer invocation, preserves host-policy-before-credential ordering, carries only a Keychain-shaped credential reference, runs exec, and returns every owned resource to zero. The existing P-256 deployed-fallback coverage remains intact.
./LOGBOOK.md:70:- GAP: The revised M0 contract requires noninteractive Ed25519 plus an approved fallback through the opaque external signer. The live adapter fixture authenticates only with an in-memory P-256 signer; the pinned C rekey probe uses file-based Ed25519 auth and therefore does not exercise the adapter/Keychain signer boundary. A scoped search for an Ed25519 live-auth credential in `LibSSH2AdapterIntegrationTests.swift` exits 1.
./LOGBOOK.md:99:- ROOT CAUSE: The generic timeout helper selected the deadline result but then drained its structured task group, so a resolver, connector, host policy, credential provider, or upload source that ignored task cancellation could hold the public operation and transport close beyond their configured deadlines.
./LOGBOOK.md:119:- REWORK: Socket-service cancellation now retires only its independently cancellable readiness operation. Channel opens may queue behind rekey under their original bounded deadline. One bounded shared rekey flight coalesces trigger reasons and lets a canceled caller detach while KEX continues. Per-channel FIFO gates retain exact write arguments across `EAGAIN`, with queued bytes still reserved against the caller cap. Returned credential algorithms must belong to both adapter capabilities and the request allowlist.
./LOGBOOK.md:120:- CONFORMANCE: Deterministic loopback regressions prove canceled and timed-out reads leave sibling channels and the session ready; an open times out while rekey is deliberately suspended and a second queued open succeeds afterward; two rekey triggers produce one KEX while the first caller cancels; four concurrent same-channel payloads survive forced bridge backpressure byte-exactly; and a supported-but-disallowed credential algorithm fails before authentication.
./LOGBOOK.md:126:- CONFORMANCE: A serialized macOS loopback fixture starts ephemeral OpenSSH with an in-memory P-256 external signer and proves host-policy-before-credential ordering, approved public-key authentication, direct-tcpip echo, concurrent exec opens, exec/stdin upload, server-rekey traffic survival, manual client rekey, keepalive transmission, five connected read/cancel races, and three successful connect/close resource baselines. The four M3 semantics remain explicit `unsupported` reports owned by `TASK-260728-3cveay`.
./LOGBOOK.md:138:- SECURITY: The adapter applies explicit KEX/host-key/cipher/MAC allowlists with no SHA-1/CBC fallback, delivers the exact raw host-key blob plus negotiated host-key method and canonical SHA-256 fingerprint to policy before credential lookup, and carries only opaque credential references/public signing material across Core. Engine prose and endpoint/user/command data never enter errors or typed diagnostics.
./LOGBOOK.md:145:- IMPLEMENTATION: `SSHDeferredSemanticReport` exposes `reported`, `notReported`, and `unsupported`. Exact receive-credit policy/snapshots, open reasons, exit metadata, KEX generation, window/server-rekey/keepalive metrics, and manual keepalive RTT now require one of those states; no default zero/false or mandatory full-fidelity policy remains. Host-before-auth, approved public-key auth, direct-tcpip, exec/stdin upload, client and server rekey handling, bounded buffers/backpressure, cancellation/lifecycle, Keychain-only credential references, keepalive transmission, available metrics, and privacy-safe errors remain mandatory.
./LOGBOOK.md:195:- DECISION: owner is **`TASK-260715-1uxx3i`** — an **existing** task. Its `macos-15` minimum lane already exists in the deployment-target policy and reading the runner's own `application.sb` is a credential-free static read inside its AC2; it already reaches this contract transitively via `TASK-260715-uyju7n`, so **no board element was created** and no edge added. `TASK-260715-1o9wjz` consumes the same answer at runtime. Tracked as **OC-5**.
./LOGBOOK.md:205:- ROOT CAUSE: the matrix has carried a prose `consumers` list since r1 and it was **never compared against the board's dependency graph**. Transitive closure over every task and bug record: 19 declared consumers, **16 reachable, 3 not** — `TASK-260715-1o9wjz`, `TASK-260715-3f4lxy`, `TASK-260715-29ws8l`. With `max_parallel=1` the scheduler was free to run all three while the matrix sat in `analysis` under its **eighth** changes-requested verdict, authoring the macOS credential resolver, the snapshot loader and the profile-trust contract against an unaccepted contract.
./LOGBOOK.md:208:- FIX: one board edge — `link(TASK-260715-29ws8l, blocked_by=TASK-260715-ypo7yo)`, cycle-checked over the whole graph first. It orders all three because 29ws8l already blocks the other two, and it is semantically real: 29ws8l **AC2** specifies "App Group and `providerConfiguration` data" and **AC4** specifies "Keychain accessibility, access group" — exactly what rules G4 and K2 decide. Authoring it without this matrix reproduces the iOS-only assumption OC-1 was raised against.
./LOGBOOK.md:213:- DECISION: rule **N1** (`numericClaimContract`), gate **R38** — the other half of the count class r6 left open. §9.1 records it biting already: r3's prose claimed a number over a list holding a different one, r4 corrected it **by hand**, r5 moved it again when K2 withdrew the keychain group. r6's scan covers App Group **record** counts only. N1 derives allowlist counts *and* the rendered key list from the rows in both artifacts. Exclusion lists are **pinned** (widening an exclusion is the quiet way to neuter a scan) and the scan's **bound is stated**: it finds `<number> keys`; two registered claims render the number without that word, so registration found them, not the scan.
./LOGBOOK.md:251:- FINDING: it was never one string. Sweeping **every** string in the r5 JSON for stale grant/count claims turned up a second survivor the reviewer had not named — `appGroupStyleRule.statement`'s "one App Group record **per family**", true of r4's two records and false since r5 deleted the probe record. Two independent instances in one artifact is a class, not a typo.
./LOGBOOK.md:254:- FINDING (**the new rule earned its keep on first run**): R32's record-count scan failed immediately on `appGroupLeastPrivilegeRule.ceremonyCostIsZero` — "**the two App Group records** are allocated at that sitting" — a **third** instance neither the reviewer nor r5 had spotted. A gate whose first run finds something its author did not believe was there is worth more than one that confirms what he already believed.
./LOGBOOK.md:266:- DECISION: `com.apple.security.application-groups` is now **prohibited on the iOS probe pair** (verdict 04 F3). r4 granted it because the probe "exercises the same shared-container path production depends on" — but `TASK-260715-1jckn0` scopes the probe to the `NETunnelProviderManager` lifecycle and versioned app messaging, its AC3 puts the whole exchange on `sendProviderMessage`, and no shared container appears anywhere. Rule G4 gained **`probeRule`**: a probe inherits **identifiers and profile classes, never entitlements**; if a probe is meant to prove a capability can be *provisioned*, that is a verification deliverable and must be an acceptance criterion **first** — the entitlement follows the criterion, not the reverse.
./LOGBOOK.md:267:- DECISION: `group.works.relux.tunnel.probe` had exactly two consumers and both lost the grant, so **the record is deleted**, not deferred — a portal record no target reads is surface with no reader. Rule **G3** restated to the stronger property it now has: *no probe carries an App Group at all*, disposability by construction rather than by careful naming. One App Group record remains.
./LOGBOOK.md:270:- SCOPE: `TASK-260728-q5kjta` (Ceremony C1) scope + AC4 corrected on the board (verdict 04 F2): four macOS App IDs not a singular pair, Network Extensions only, **zero** App Group records/capabilities, **no** Keychain mutation (there is no portal record), Mac Development profile class, all four iOS App IDs excluded. A downstream-consequence paragraph in the matrix is not the contract the operator reads.
./LOGBOOK.md:275:- DECISION: `application-groups` is now **prohibited on all four macOS targets** (production *and* probe) and `required` on all four iOS targets — new rule **G4**: an App Group is granted only where a *currently selected* mechanism uses one of the grants the entitlement actually confers. Reopening conditions are deliberately **grant-specific** (group-prefixed Mach service / group-prefixed POSIX IPC / provider moved into a user context), so "the spec mentions App Groups" or "a shipping product carries it" cannot reopen the row.
./LOGBOOK.md:276:- FINDING: What the entitlement grants on macOS is readable, not arguable. `/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb` (26.5/25F71) contains exactly two `sandbox-array-entitlement` blocks for the key — lines **302-330** and **459-465** — conferring six grants. Three are **home-relative** and therefore break across the root/user split (group container r/w; `network-bind`/`network-outbound` inside it; `~/Library/Application Scripts/<suite>`). Three are **system-wide and do work across it**: `/Library/Application Support/AppStore/GroupContent/<suite>`, `mach-lookup`/`mach-register` on `global-name-prefix "<suite>."` (**lines 326-328** — the XPC channel, and the only reason the macOS group ever looked load-bearing), and `ipc-posix*` on `ipc-posix-name-prefix "<suite>/"` (**line 329** — POSIX shm, which no candidate ever proposed). "App Groups do nothing on macOS" would be the tidy version and it is false; the verdict rests on *these grants are unused*, not on *the entitlement is inert*.
./LOGBOOK.md:279:- DECISION: **Ceremony C1 shrank.** No App Group record is created and the App Groups capability is enabled on no App ID. The convenience argument did not need weighing — it evaporates: C1's `explicitlyNotAuthorized` already excludes all four iOS App IDs under ADR-024, so iOS provisioning *already* needs a later portal sitting, and those four iOS App IDs are the records' only consumers. New rule **R30** makes C1 scope **derived** from what its App IDs require, so growing or shrinking it by hand cannot pass.
./LOGBOOK.md:286:- DECISION: Revocation is now specified as a **five-state table**, not a yes/no. Synchronous when the configuration is installed and enabled (running or not — the send launches it); unavailable and deferred to a start-time sweep when the configuration is disabled (`NEVPNErrorConfigurationDisabled`), invalid/unapproved (`NEVPNErrorConfigurationInvalid`), or when no host is running at all. Seeding moved out of `startTunnel` entirely, so the credential is normally at rest before any start; `startTunnel` is now read-only and fails fast with `credentialNotProvisioned`. The start-time sweep survives as crash-recovery defence, not as the deletion path.
./LOGBOOK.md:293:### 0822 — macOS credential transport decided: the root provider owns a System keychain item, seeded once over the NE app-message channel (TASK-260728-7ii1xz)
./LOGBOOK.md:298:- FINDING: **The App Group channel is broken on macOS too**, not just the keychain. `containerURL(forSecurityApplicationGroupIdentifier:)` resolves relative to the caller's home and the root provider's home is `/private/var/root` (verified root-private, `ls` exit 1). `.spec/architecture.md:111` says shared state is "an App Group configuration snapshot and Keychain references" — both halves fail on macOS. The non-secret snapshot must travel in `providerConfiguration`, which the accepted M1 lifecycle contract already does. Directly affects `TASK-260715-3f4lxy`.
./LOGBOOK.md:300:- FINDING: System keychain **attributes are world-readable** — `security dump-keychain /Library/Keychains/System.keychain` as uid 502 exits 0 and prints 192 items' attributes. Item *data* is ACL-gated (`-d` blocked on an interactive authorization prompt, killed at 120s). Consequence: the credential item's service/account values must carry no hostname, account, or profile name.
./LOGBOOK.md:322:### 0722 — iOS-style App Group IDs are the correct macOS choice since Feb 2025, with one unverified corner (TASK-260715-ypo7yo, r2)
./LOGBOOK.md:325:- ANOMALY: Every Apple example of an app group used as a sandboxed **Mach service name prefix**, and all three shipping products inspected here, use the macOS-style Team-ID form — and all predate Feb 2025. No source found confirms an iOS-style ID works as a Mach service prefix. Not blocking: the spec's host↔provider channel is an App Group snapshot plus Keychain references (`.spec/architecture.md:111`), not XPC. Recorded as **OC-4** owned by `TASK-260728-7ii1xz`, with the amendment path being a separate *unregistered* macOS-style ID for that one purpose. This supersedes the 0657 claim below that the macOS group is required "as the XPC Mach-service namespace" — that purpose is now conditional, not established.
./LOGBOOK.md:332:- VERIFICATION: 24 rules / 537 checks over the JSON, exit 0, plus **23 negative gates** that each re-introduce a defect and must fail closed naming their rule (6 for the NE-channel defect, 4 App Group, 3 allowlist, 3 sandbox attribution, 7 re-checking preserved r1 decisions). The r1 harness mutated the JSON with regex substitution, which can silently no-op; r2 mutates the parsed object, so a mutation that fails to apply is itself reported as a gate failure.
./LOGBOOK.md:336:- FINDING: The same root/user split kills the App Group **container** as a macOS host↔provider channel: the sysex's container resolves under `/var/root/Library/Group Containers`, the host's under the user's home. App Groups are still **required** on macOS, but only as the XPC Mach-service namespace a sandboxed process must prefix with — not as shared storage. `TASK-260715-3f4lxy` (profile snapshot loader) inherits this.
./LOGBOOK.md:337:- DECISION: The matrix withholds `keychain-access-groups` from `works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel` (`prohibited-pending-decision`) rather than provisioning a right the provider cannot exercise. Created `TASK-260728-7ii1xz` to select the macOS credential transport and return a grant-or-keep-prohibited amendment; it blocks `379cpk`, `1o9wjz`, `29ws8l`, `3f4lxy`. Five credential tasks all assumed the shared-group model and none owned the question of whether it holds.
./LOGBOOK.md:353:- ROOT CAUSE: `TunnelProviderAdapter.providerDidFail` was `nonisolated` and deferred *all* admission into a freshly spawned unstructured `Task`. Swift orders nothing between unstructured tasks contending for the same actor, so two sequential provider-failure callbacks could enter `handleProviderFailure` in reverse order. The `cancelTunnelIssuedGeneration` ledger enforced exactly-once but had no notion of *which* call arrived first, so the second error (`internalInvariant` 1009) beat the first (`runtimeStartupFailed` 1007) in **10 of 40** focused runs (~25%).
./LOGBOOK.md:366:- DECISION: `TASK-260715-39xz9g` is no longer a conditional hold. A read-only `BatchMode` probe against the owner-authorized alias authenticated without a prompt (remote reports Darwin); no hostname, address, username, key path, or credential was recorded. The probe is explicitly *not* conformance evidence — raw pre-auth host-key evidence is still owed.
./LOGBOOK.md:375:- FINDING: The notarization credential was described as "available" while existing only as a mode-0600 `.p8` file — the project invariant is Keychain-only. ADR-025 now requires a named `notarytool` Keychain profile, a real authentication check, and a recorded disposition for the source file; `TASK-260728-dveo1o` owns it and blocks `3gkwn0`.
./LOGBOOK.md:382:> Superseded by the 0655 entry: the 202-task claim, the single-session Ceremony C1, and the "notary credentials exist" readiness statement were all corrected.
./LOGBOOK.md:456:- RESIDUAL: Production SSH selection (`TASK-260715-1gjxer`), DNS runtime authorization (`TASK-260721-3miqh4`), physical route/DNS evidence, credentials/entitlements, relay composition, update/release controls, and independent candidate review remain explicit evidence gates.
./LOGBOOK.md:469:### 1921 — Portable relay builds use one pinned, offline, credential-isolated toolchain (TASK-260715-27uz4n)
./LOGBOOK.md:471:- SECURITY: Target builds construct an environment allowlist and isolate `HOME`, `TMPDIR`, `GOCACHE`, `GOMODCACHE`, and `GOPATH` per target below `.build/relay/work`; `GOTOOLCHAIN=local`, `GOPROXY=off`, `GOSUMDB=off`, `GOVCS=off`, `GOENV=off`, and `GOWORK=off` prevent undeclared downloads or workstation credential/config reuse.
./LOGBOOK.md:572:- IMPLEMENTATION: The authoritative standard-library-only relay module now pins Go 1.26.5 and builds `relux-relay` plus `relux-relay-protocol-test` for Darwin/Linux amd64/arm64. Both expose deterministic credential-free metadata smoke output; the protocol-test entry point runs only empty health and one generated-schema-backed version mismatch, and the relay explicitly reports that feature behavior is not implemented.
./LOGBOOK.md:574:- PROVENANCE REWORK: Release entry points now require offline-provisioned official Go and Syft archives retained beside their installed tools. They recheck the accepted host-specific archive hash, canonical path-free receipt, installed archive members, exact Go 1.26.5 host identity with `GOTOOLCHAIN=local`, and Syft 1.48.0 commit `3e2bc6ed095f7ec1a415fb38cfe1c319e95dfed6`; missing, older/newer, wrong-platform, wrong-commit, wrong-checksum, auto-toolchain, and version-only inputs fail closed.
./LOGBOOK.md:578:- PRIVACY: The board runner's automatically attached raw spawn log was removed from the versionable outcome resources. Persistent evidence contains no raw process log, host identifier, absolute local path, credential, payload, or remote-controlled string.
./LOGBOOK.md:608:- DIAGNOSTICS: Runtime schema v1 is additively extended to 139 fixed counters, 75 fixed gauges, and two 12-bucket cumulative histograms. Registry-owned current gauges publish through a generation-scoped fixed-size atomic coalescer and reconcile into snapshots independently of the bounded event lane, so dropped counter/histogram ingestion remains observable without leaving phantom flow/open/channel/byte gauges. The TCP surface contains only aggregate pressure/terminal tokens, counts, bytes, health, and channel-open latency; it accepts no destination, hostname, address, port, payload, credential, flow/channel ID, or free-form label. Maximum populated encoding is 13,568 bytes of the 65,536-byte limit and generation advancement isolates stale publications.
./LOGBOOK.md:626:- RESOURCE: Adapter capacity is an atomic non-waiting reservation bounded by the HEV session ceiling and the measured M0/SSH/Core memory equation; exact flow, chunk, queue, window, and timeout values remain injected measurement inputs for M1/M3 rather than promoted constants. Diagnostics are aggregate schema-v1 only and exclude credentials, SOCKS bytes, endpoints, hostnames, payloads, and flow identifiers.
./LOGBOOK.md:630:- FIX: Both iOS and macOS `VPNHostSession` adapters now catch synchronous `NETunnelProviderSession.startTunnel(options:)` errors and translate them through the existing `VPNPreferencePlatformError` model before crossing into `VPNSessionController`. Public `NEVPNError.configurationInvalid`, `.configurationDisabled`, and `.connectionFailed` retain their stable results; every other `NSError` retains its original domain and integer code.
./LOGBOOK.md:637:- REGRESSION: `IOSVPNHostSession.startTunnel` and `MacOSVPNHostSession.startTunnel` forward the public `NETunnelProviderSession.startTunnel(options:)` thrown `NSError` unchanged, while `VPNSessionController.performStart` maps only `VPNPreferencePlatformError` / `VPNPlatformError` and otherwise erases the real domain/code to reflected type plus code 0. The unit test injects a pre-normalized fake error, so the production adapter path is untested and cannot satisfy the accepted exact synchronous `NEVPNError` mapping.
./LOGBOOK.md:641:- DECISION: `OwnedVPNManagerRepository` remains the only ownership/schema authority and now hands out a session only from a freshly loaded exact-owned current manager. `VPNSessionController` retains no manager cache or forwarding state; iOS/macOS wrappers retain the fresh manager only to keep its exact public `NETunnelProviderSession` alive for the command/observer lifetime.
./LOGBOOK.md:668:- IDENTITY: The complete host/provider/App Group/Keychain `PlatformVPNIdentity` remains injectable for deterministic fixtures. Both production factories fail closed until `TASK-260715-ypo7yo` supplies accepted signed bindings; no guessed identifier landed.
./LOGBOOK.md:679:- DECISION: A manager is owned only when its protocol is `NETunnelProviderProtocol`, its provider bundle ID exactly matches the accepted platform binding, and its stable provider-configuration marker matches. Labels, server address, enabled state, profile UUID, schema version, and array order never establish ownership. Every write/remove rechecks a freshly loaded object; unmarked lookalikes and duplicates are zero-write conflicts outside explicit inactive duplicate repair.
./LOGBOOK.md:681:- AUTHORITY: `NETunnelProviderSession.status` is the sole system-session authority; a fresh generation/sequence provider snapshot is the sole runtime capability authority. Host suspension/termination removes observers only. Provider start/stop once-gates and exact deadlines remain extension-owned and join the accepted coordinator cleanup.
./LOGBOOK.md:682:- RESIDUAL: Exact four-target bundle IDs, App Group, Keychain group, and entitlements are not yet accepted (`TASK-260715-ypo7yo`). Shared implementation proceeds through injected identities, while production composition fails closed instead of inventing values. Human ratification stays decoupled in `TASK-260717-1dsqnj`.
./LOGBOOK.md:719:- PRIVACY: The default schema has no lane/run/flow IDs, DNS or destination fields, addresses, credentials, commands/stdin, payload content, or traffic samples. State transition times are monotonic process-uptime milliseconds; errors retain only validated domain/code; flow, memory, route, health, packet/byte, drop-reason, DNS-result, and SSH values are aggregates.
./LOGBOOK.md:732:- SECURITY: The prior `TunnelConfiguration.parameters: [String:String]` escape hatch made private-key/passphrase exclusion structurally impossible. It was removed; provider/start configuration now contains only a schema-v1 UUID-backed profile reference, while configuration snapshots use distinct UUID-backed profile revision, credential, and trust references. Harness experiment parameters remain in harness-only recorded output and no longer cross into the runtime configuration.
./LOGBOOK.md:944:- BLOCKER: The neutral credential is an opaque async signer (`SSHPublicKeyCredential`), but ReluxNIOSSH public-key auth accepts only concrete `NIOSSHPrivateKey` and signs synchronously inside an internal message initializer. The adapter cannot authenticate without a candidate downcast/private-key export or a new external-signer fork API.
./LOGBOOK.md:972:- DECISION: Both engine adapters use one serialized, type-erased `ReluxTunnelCore` boundary with mandatory raw host-key policy evaluation before credential access, bounded partial writes, independent byte-stream halves, capped observable per-channel receive credit, automatic byte/time plus explicit/server rekey, keepalive, stable errors, schema-v1 metrics/events, and privacy-safe opaque lane identity. ADR-014 remains open; unsupported mandatory behavior is a red matrix row, never a candidate-specific skip.
./LOGBOOK.md:998:- DECISION: HEV's upstream SOCKS connection is restricted to an IPv4-loopback listener with fresh per-run RFC 1929 credentials. Only an authenticated owned channel reaches the injected adapter; pending-authentication count and timeout are caller inputs, and an external no-auth ingress test is rejected before the seam.
